{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794476951070,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"id":"prt_1cf294619001CsxC6zTjT4ZwFf","messageID":"msg_1cf2935aa001gmAWh50bySX0oW","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","snapshot":"df9c56c2add0dcfbad5931e996fb8d6c2442a838","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794476954291,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_r0rgkjpapwfqb3pr8hm06rpm","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n --no-heading \"hand-roll|hand rolled|handrolled|improvise|improvised|ad hoc|ad-hoc\" specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/002-routing-perfection-research .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --glob '*.md' | head -30","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:34:The repository ships 45 slash commands that own complete workflows, but the skill advisor tells the model about skills, not commands. Only `/doctor:*` commands reach the model as a runnable line. Every other command arrives as a raw bridge id such as `command-spec-kit`, or not at all, and no rule says whether the model should run a surfaced command or ask first. A user who chats without typing a command therefore gets a hand-rolled approach where a command workflow exists.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:69:6. **Chat-only users.** Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow: the advisor brief, a gate in `AGENTS.md`, a hook, the skill pointing at its command, or a refusal in the runner. Include the evidence for workflows being hand-rolled today.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:98:| REQ-001 | The research runs through the `/deep:research:auto` workflow as a three-lineage fan-out, never a hand-rolled loop | `ls research/lineages` lists three lineage directories and each holds iteration files |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/goal.md:45:| D1 | The research runs through the `/deep:research:auto` workflow as a fan-out, never a hand-rolled loop |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:98:- I expected a global rule about commands somewhere in the ruleset; grepping `hand-roll` found only the substitution prohibition, which assumes the workflow is named. That absence is itself the finding. (iteration 6)\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/research.md:104:- The ruleset already forbids hand-rolling a named workflow (`PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK`, sk-git rule 16); what it lacks is the discovery step.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-009.md:28:7. Design implication, and the reason this iteration matters: the binding layer cannot assume a brief arrives. On Cursor and Devin the reliable surfaces are the static rule and `AGENTS.md`; the advisor CLI is callable from the model's own shell on every runtime. So the one-line rule from iteration 6 should be phrased to work when the brief is absent — \"if no brief arrived, ask the advisor before hand-rolling\" — and the command pointer should be generated into the static rule rather than hand-copied into it [INFERENCE: from the dormant Cursor rule and the root-doc precedence established in iteration 6].\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:12:4. Grepped the ruleset for existing anti-hand-rolling language as evidence of the failure being known.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:24:5. Runner refusal exists only as normative prose, not enforcement: the deep-research skill says the loop is \"invoked exclusively through `/deep:research:auto` or `/deep:research:confirm`\" and never simulated \"with ad hoc shell dispatch, nested CLI loops, direct `@deep-research` Task dispatch\" [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/SKILL.md:84]. A refusal only fires once that SKILL.md is in context, which is after the hand-roll decision would already have been made.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:26:6. The ruleset already treats hand-rolling as a known failure mode: `AGENTS.md` carries PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK (\"NEVER silently hand-roll a substitute for a plan-named purpose-built workflow\") and sk-git rule 16 forbids hand-rolling the publish path [SOURCE: AGENTS.md:13]. The gap the rules leave is discovery: they forbid substituting a known workflow, and say nothing about how a model learns the workflow exists when the user typed prose.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:83:- What did not work and why: I expected a global rule about commands somewhere in the ruleset; grepping `hand-roll` found only the substitution prohibition, which assumes the workflow is named. That absence is itself the finding.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:40:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today?\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md:71:Angle 6 (chat-only users): which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:54:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today? [legacy-import]\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:64:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today?\n","metadata":{"output":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:34:The repository ships 45 slash commands that own complete workflows, but the skill advisor tells the model about skills, not commands. Only `/doctor:*` commands reach the model as a runnable line. Every other command arrives as a raw bridge id such as `command-spec-kit`, or not at all, and no rule says whether the model should run a surfaced command or ask first. A user who chats without typing a command therefore gets a hand-rolled approach where a command workflow exists.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:69:6. **Chat-only users.** Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow: the advisor brief, a gate in `AGENTS.md`, a hook, the skill pointing at its command, or a refusal in the runner. Include the evidence for workflows being hand-rolled today.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/spec.md:98:| REQ-001 | The research runs through the `/deep:research:auto` workflow as a three-lineage fan-out, never a hand-rolled loop | `ls research/lineages` lists three lineage directories and each holds iteration files |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/goal.md:45:| D1 | The research runs through the `/deep:research:auto` workflow as a fan-out, never a hand-rolled loop |\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:98:- I expected a global rule about commands somewhere in the ruleset; grepping `hand-roll` found only the substitution prohibition, which assumes the workflow is named. That absence is itself the finding. (iteration 6)\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/research.md:104:- The ruleset already forbids hand-rolling a named workflow (`PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK`, sk-git rule 16); what it lacks is the discovery step.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-009.md:28:7. Design implication, and the reason this iteration matters: the binding layer cannot assume a brief arrives. On Cursor and Devin the reliable surfaces are the static rule and `AGENTS.md`; the advisor CLI is callable from the model's own shell on every runtime. So the one-line rule from iteration 6 should be phrased to work when the brief is absent — \"if no brief arrived, ask the advisor before hand-rolling\" — and the command pointer should be generated into the static rule rather than hand-copied into it [INFERENCE: from the dormant Cursor rule and the root-doc precedence established in iteration 6].\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:12:4. Grepped the ruleset for existing anti-hand-rolling language as evidence of the failure being known.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:24:5. Runner refusal exists only as normative prose, not enforcement: the deep-research skill says the loop is \"invoked exclusively through `/deep:research:auto` or `/deep:research:confirm`\" and never simulated \"with ad hoc shell dispatch, nested CLI loops, direct `@deep-research` Task dispatch\" [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/SKILL.md:84]. A refusal only fires once that SKILL.md is in context, which is after the hand-roll decision would already have been made.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:26:6. The ruleset already treats hand-rolling as a known failure mode: `AGENTS.md` carries PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK (\"NEVER silently hand-roll a substitute for a plan-named purpose-built workflow\") and sk-git rule 16 forbids hand-rolling the publish path [SOURCE: AGENTS.md:13]. The gap the rules leave is discovery: they forbid substituting a known workflow, and say nothing about how a model learns the workflow exists when the user typed prose.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/pi-deepseek/iterations/iteration-006.md:83:- What did not work and why: I expected a global rule about commands somewhere in the ruleset; grepping `hand-roll` found only the substitution prohibition, which assumes the workflow is named. That absence is itself the finding.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:40:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today?\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/iterations/iteration-005.md:71:Angle 6 (chat-only users): which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today.\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:54:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today? [legacy-import]\nspecs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement/research/lineages/opencode-deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:64:- [ ] Which mechanism brings a request typed as plain chat onto the command workflow, and what is the evidence that workflows are hand-rolled today?\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -n --no-heading \"hand-roll|hand rolled|handrolled|improvise|improvised|ad hoc|ad-hoc\" specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/007-command-surfacing-and-workflow-enforcement specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/002-routing-perfection-research .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor --glob '*.md' | head -30","time":{"start":1794476954240,"end":1794476954289}},"id":"prt_1cf295025001tRN50Qpd6EFXxp","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","messageID":"msg_1cf2935aa001gmAWh50bySX0oW"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794476954615,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_nm3qulxbt5wb7viyptivuzci","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,90p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md; echo \"---HOOK-CONTRACT---\"; sed -n '1,45p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"---\nname: system-skill-advisor\ndescription: Routes non-trivial requests to matching skills through the daemon-backed advisor CLI and stable advisor command ids.\nallowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Glob, Grep]\nversion: 0.18.7.0\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"skill advisor\"\n  - \"gate 2 routing\"\n  - \"advisor_recommend\"\n  - \"advisor_status\"\n  - \"which skill should handle this\"\n  - \"route this request to a skill\"\n  - \"system skill advisor\"\nkeywords:\n  - skill-routing\n  - gate-2\n  - advisor-cli\n  - legacy-tool-bridge\n  - skill-graph\n  - semantic-lane\nintent_signals:\n  - \"Gate 2 skill routing\"\n  - \"advisor_recommend CLI routing\"\n  - \"advisor_status freshness check\"\n  - \"which skill should handle this request\"\n  - \"run the skill advisor hook\"\n---\n\n<!-- Keywords: system-skill-advisor, skill-advisor, advisor_recommend, advisor_status, advisor_rebuild, advisor_validate, skill_graph_scan, skill_graph_query, skill_graph_status, skill_graph_validate, skill_graph_propagate_enhances, cross-skill-edges, gate-2-routing, cli-front-door, daemon-cli, legacy-tool-bridge, skill-graph -->\n\n# System Skill Advisor\n\nRoutes non-trivial user requests to the right skill through the standalone Skill Advisor package and its CLI front door.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill when the work is about skill selection, Gate 2 routing, advisor CLI commands, prompt-time skill-advisor hooks, skill graph freshness or the standalone advisor package.\n\nActivation signals:\n\n- A request asks which skill should handle a task.\n- A runtime hook needs a skill recommendation before execution.\n- An operator asks about `advisor_recommend`, `advisor_status`, `advisor_rebuild`, `advisor_validate`, `skill_graph_scan`, `skill_graph_query`, `skill_graph_status`, `skill_graph_validate` or `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`.\n- A packet touches the skill graph, skill metadata, advisor scorer, advisor feature catalog or manual testing playbook.\n\nDo not use this skill as a replacement for the recommended target skill. For example, route code implementation to `sk-code`, documentation authoring to `sk-doc`, git work to `sk-git` and MCP orchestration to `mcp-code-mode` after the advisor has made the recommendation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nUse this skill for non-trivial Gate 2 routing or requests about advisor commands, hooks, skill graph or the package.\nAn exact skill name or explicit user direction takes priority.\nOtherwise, use `advisor_recommend` through the CLI to choose a target skill.\nLoad that target skill before acting in its domain.\nThis package covers advisor routing and behavior.\nFor local documentation, load the leaf mapped to the detected intent.\nWeak signals use fallback references. Broad browsing uses a package index.\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\nThe advisor runs as a resident daemon with one CLI front door. The package owns the whole routing boundary, so routing can be tuned and rolled back independently.\n\nThe package owns the advisor daemon and its CLI front door, and it lists all **9 commands**. The command ids stay stable:\n\nCommands (9):\n\n- `advisor_recommend`\n- `advisor_rebuild`\n- `advisor_status`\n- `advisor_validate`\n- `skill_graph_scan`\n- `skill_graph_query`\n- `skill_graph_status`\n- `skill_graph_validate`\n- `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`\n\n`skill_graph_propagate_enhances` is trust-gated only for real apply writes (`mode=apply` with `dryRun` not `true`). Report, propose and dry-run apply calls remain read-safe.\n\nThe stable command ids matter because live consumers already call them from hooks, the Python scorer, doctor workflows, scripts and CI. The package boundary supplies the isolation, so callers use the advisor without learning a new vocabulary.\n\nThe CLI is the only front door. The same 9 commands are callable through `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command>` over the daemon's unix socket. A shim refuses to run a stale dist.\nRecovery example: `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"<request>\"}' --format json --timeout-ms 3000`.\nExit codes:\n- `0`: success.\n---HOOK-CONTRACT---\n---\ntitle: Skill Advisor Hook Reference\ndescription: Operator contract for the maintained Skill Advisor adapters across Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, and the OpenCode plugin. Surfaces a prompt-safe routing brief on each user prompt; advisory only and fail-open.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"skill advisor hook reference\"\n  - \"native-first advisor hooks\"\n  - \"advisor hook runtime matrix\"\n  - \"opencode plugin bridge\"\n  - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 3.6.4.0\n---\n\n# Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThe Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n\nThe maintained advisor implementation lives in `system-skill-advisor/runtime/`. The four editor runtimes (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin) each carry a thin `user-prompt-submit` shim in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/` that resolves and spawns the compiled advisor in `system-skill-advisor`. The real brief-building, scoring, freshness, and rendering logic lives in the advisor package. Pi carries a native TypeScript extension that imports the same advisor module in-process (Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old two-process blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send). OpenCode integrates through the committed plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`, which shells out to the advisor CLI.\n\nThe single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n\nPaths beginning with `runtime/` in the package-local tables resolve from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/` (for the shims) or `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/` (for the advisor). Paths beginning with `.skilled/` are repository-root relative.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n\nOn each user-prompt event the adapter:\n\n1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so the daemon skips compiled-route enrichment and starts no `compiled-route.cjs` child. A daemon still running an older build rejects that option with JSON-RPC `-32602` and the CLI retries once without it.\n5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n\nThe injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n\n---\n","metadata":{"output":"---\nname: system-skill-advisor\ndescription: Routes non-trivial requests to matching skills through the daemon-backed advisor CLI and stable advisor command ids.\nallowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Glob, Grep]\nversion: 0.18.7.0\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"skill advisor\"\n  - \"gate 2 routing\"\n  - \"advisor_recommend\"\n  - \"advisor_status\"\n  - \"which skill should handle this\"\n  - \"route this request to a skill\"\n  - \"system skill advisor\"\nkeywords:\n  - skill-routing\n  - gate-2\n  - advisor-cli\n  - legacy-tool-bridge\n  - skill-graph\n  - semantic-lane\nintent_signals:\n  - \"Gate 2 skill routing\"\n  - \"advisor_recommend CLI routing\"\n  - \"advisor_status freshness check\"\n  - \"which skill should handle this request\"\n  - \"run the skill advisor hook\"\n---\n\n<!-- Keywords: system-skill-advisor, skill-advisor, advisor_recommend, advisor_status, advisor_rebuild, advisor_validate, skill_graph_scan, skill_graph_query, skill_graph_status, skill_graph_validate, skill_graph_propagate_enhances, cross-skill-edges, gate-2-routing, cli-front-door, daemon-cli, legacy-tool-bridge, skill-graph -->\n\n# System Skill Advisor\n\nRoutes non-trivial user requests to the right skill through the standalone Skill Advisor package and its CLI front door.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill when the work is about skill selection, Gate 2 routing, advisor CLI commands, prompt-time skill-advisor hooks, skill graph freshness or the standalone advisor package.\n\nActivation signals:\n\n- A request asks which skill should handle a task.\n- A runtime hook needs a skill recommendation before execution.\n- An operator asks about `advisor_recommend`, `advisor_status`, `advisor_rebuild`, `advisor_validate`, `skill_graph_scan`, `skill_graph_query`, `skill_graph_status`, `skill_graph_validate` or `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`.\n- A packet touches the skill graph, skill metadata, advisor scorer, advisor feature catalog or manual testing playbook.\n\nDo not use this skill as a replacement for the recommended target skill. For example, route code implementation to `sk-code`, documentation authoring to `sk-doc`, git work to `sk-git` and MCP orchestration to `mcp-code-mode` after the advisor has made the recommendation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nUse this skill for non-trivial Gate 2 routing or requests about advisor commands, hooks, skill graph or the package.\nAn exact skill name or explicit user direction takes priority.\nOtherwise, use `advisor_recommend` through the CLI to choose a target skill.\nLoad that target skill before acting in its domain.\nThis package covers advisor routing and behavior.\nFor local documentation, load the leaf mapped to the detected intent.\nWeak signals use fallback references. Broad browsing uses a package index.\nRead [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\nThe advisor runs as a resident daemon with one CLI front door. The package owns the whole routing boundary, so routing can be tuned and rolled back independently.\n\nThe package owns the advisor daemon and its CLI front door, and it lists all **9 commands**. The command ids stay stable:\n\nCommands (9):\n\n- `advisor_recommend`\n- `advisor_rebuild`\n- `advisor_status`\n- `advisor_validate`\n- `skill_graph_scan`\n- `skill_graph_query`\n- `skill_graph_status`\n- `skill_graph_validate`\n- `skill_graph_propagate_enhances`\n\n`skill_graph_propagate_enhances` is trust-gated only for real apply writes (`mode=apply` with `dryRun` not `true`). Report, propose and dry-run apply calls remain read-safe.\n\nThe stable command ids matter because live consumers already call them from hooks, the Python scorer, doctor workflows, scripts and CI. The package boundary supplies the isolation, so callers use the advisor without learning a new vocabulary.\n\nThe CLI is the only front door. The same 9 commands are callable through `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs <command>` over the daemon's unix socket. A shim refuses to run a stale dist.\nRecovery example: `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json '{\"prompt\":\"<request>\"}' --format json --timeout-ms 3000`.\nExit codes:\n- `0`: success.\n---HOOK-CONTRACT---\n---\ntitle: Skill Advisor Hook Reference\ndescription: Operator contract for the maintained Skill Advisor adapters across Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, and the OpenCode plugin. Surfaces a prompt-safe routing brief on each user prompt; advisory only and fail-open.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"skill advisor hook reference\"\n  - \"native-first advisor hooks\"\n  - \"advisor hook runtime matrix\"\n  - \"opencode plugin bridge\"\n  - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 3.6.4.0\n---\n\n# Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\nThe Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n\nThe maintained advisor implementation lives in `system-skill-advisor/runtime/`. The four editor runtimes (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin) each carry a thin `user-prompt-submit` shim in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/` that resolves and spawns the compiled advisor in `system-skill-advisor`. The real brief-building, scoring, freshness, and rendering logic lives in the advisor package. Pi carries a native TypeScript extension that imports the same advisor module in-process (Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old two-process blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send). OpenCode integrates through the committed plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`, which shells out to the advisor CLI.\n\nThe single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n\nPaths beginning with `runtime/` in the package-local tables resolve from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/` (for the shims) or `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/` (for the advisor). Paths beginning with `.skilled/` are repository-root relative.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n\nOn each user-prompt event the adapter:\n\n1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: false`, so the daemon skips compiled-route enrichment and starts no `compiled-route.cjs` child. A daemon still running an older build rejects that option with JSON-RPC `-32602` and the CLI retries once without it.\n5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n\nThe injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n\n---\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"sed -n '1,90p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md; echo \"---HOOK-CONTRACT---\"; sed -n '1,45p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","time":{"start":1794476954584,"end":1794476954613}},"id":"prt_1cf29529a00163qhIt3MTngaAW","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","messageID":"msg_1cf2935aa001gmAWh50bySX0oW"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794476956102,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"id":"prt_1cf2959c3001UFRuVWobgtfpYz","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"90a8e18e557e59a04e89b92783cb3b2db5ed93bd","messageID":"msg_1cf2935aa001gmAWh50bySX0oW","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":232278,"input":549,"output":294,"reasoning":395,"cache":{"write":0,"read":231040}},"cost":0.00118887}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794476960552,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"id":"prt_1cf296b24001Xv5P3m0jwcW4id","messageID":"msg_1cf295e23001HdT4mgFPSOC7BA","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","snapshot":"90a8e18e557e59a04e89b92783cb3b2db5ed93bd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794476967082,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_pibbrb7fyo461l8mfsxx2yj6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -n \"PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK\\|NEVER silently hand-roll\" AGENTS.md | head -4; grep -n \"A doctor command appears\\|recommending the matching skill\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md; grep -n \"is invoked exclusively\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/SKILL.md","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"20:#### PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK — HARD BLOCKER (cannot be overridden)\n27:3. **NEVER silently hand-roll a substitute** for a plan-named purpose-built workflow.\n68:- **Artifact trigger — binds on what you write, regardless of the advisor score.** Before the FIRST code write, route through `sk-code`. Before the FIRST `.md` write, route through `sk-doc`, except spec-folder docs (`system-spec-kit`'s) and the gitignored runtime state Gate 3 exempts, which no skill owns. Routing means loading what its ROUTER.md resolves, per Gate 5's loading rule. A skill already in context is not re-read. A resolved contract wrong for this case is followed and amended, per PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK step 4.\n188:- **Do not ask permission to continue an already-approved, in-scope step.** This never waives a mandatory wait: Gate 3, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push go-ahead and the blast-radius stop-for-yes all still block. The §2 child-dispatch exemption is the one exception.\n21:The Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n43:The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n84:This skill is invoked exclusively through `/deep:research:auto` or `/deep:research:confirm` -- the command YAML owns state, dispatch, convergence, and synthesis. Never simulate the loop with ad hoc shell dispatch, nested CLI loops, direct `@deep-research` Task dispatch, `/tmp` prompt files, or state outside the resolved local research packet.\n","metadata":{"output":"20:#### PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK — HARD BLOCKER (cannot be overridden)\n27:3. **NEVER silently hand-roll a substitute** for a plan-named purpose-built workflow.\n68:- **Artifact trigger — binds on what you write, regardless of the advisor score.** Before the FIRST code write, route through `sk-code`. Before the FIRST `.md` write, route through `sk-doc`, except spec-folder docs (`system-spec-kit`'s) and the gitignored runtime state Gate 3 exempts, which no skill owns. Routing means loading what its ROUTER.md resolves, per Gate 5's loading rule. A skill already in context is not re-read. A resolved contract wrong for this case is followed and amended, per PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK step 4.\n188:- **Do not ask permission to continue an already-approved, in-scope step.** This never waives a mandatory wait: Gate 3, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push go-ahead and the blast-radius stop-for-yes all still block. The §2 child-dispatch exemption is the one exception.\n21:The Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n43:The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the recommended skill(s) with their confidence. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n84:This skill is invoked exclusively through `/deep:research:auto` or `/deep:research:confirm` -- the command YAML owns state, dispatch, convergence, and synthesis. Never simulate the loop with ad hoc shell dispatch, nested CLI loops, direct `@deep-research` Task dispatch, `/tmp` prompt files, or state outside the resolved local research packet.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n \"PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK\\|NEVER silently hand-roll\" AGENTS.md | head -4; grep -n \"A doctor command appears\\|recommending the matching skill\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md; grep -n \"is invoked exclusively\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/SKILL.md","time":{"start":1794476967048,"end":1794476967080}},"id":"prt_1cf29829a001Dv2TUBhE9iDdsi","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","messageID":"msg_1cf295e23001HdT4mgFPSOC7BA"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794476967444,"sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_576uzkg1iswkiu1xdvefk8od","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"grep -rn \"command\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md | head -8; echo \"---DEEP-HUB---\"; grep -n \"command\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/SKILL.md | head -8","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":".skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:3:description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:10:<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:32:| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:78:- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:97:  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:151:- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n---DEEP-HUB---\n26:Use this skill (through the hub) for any active deep-loop workflow. Invoke it as `Skill(system-deep-loop)` (optionally with a mode hint such as `research: <request>`); the hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested mode packet. Active `/deep:*` commands and native agent types remain as complementary surfaces over the same packets.\n87:Its legacy public surfaces, the `/deep:ai-council` command and the `ai-council` agent, intentionally keep the shorter `ai-council` key.\n105:- **ALWAYS** keep advisor projection maps hardcoded and drift-guarded against the registry; command mode routing is still hardcoded in the command files and does not resolve through `mode-registry.json`.\n107:- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity, whether a mode is reached via `Skill(system-deep-loop)`, a `/deep:*` command, or an agent.\n109:- **ALWAYS** keep `Skill(system-deep-loop)` hub routing, the `/deep:*` commands, and the agent types as complementary surfaces over the same packets — never let one surface fork per-mode logic out of its packet.\n126:- Commands: the active `/deep:*` commands under `.skilled/commands/deep/` (complementary surface).\n136:- `Skill(system-deep-loop[, hint])` reaches a mode, and the `/deep:*` commands and agent types still reach the same packets.\n149:- `Skill(system-deep-loop)` is the invokable hub; active `/deep:*` commands and the agent types (`deep-research`, `deep-review`, `ai-council`) are complementary surfaces over the same packets.\n","metadata":{"output":".skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:3:description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:10:<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:32:| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:78:- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:97:  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md:151:- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n---DEEP-HUB---\n26:Use this skill (through the hub) for any active deep-loop workflow. Invoke it as `Skill(system-deep-loop)` (optionally with a mode hint such as `research: <request>`); the hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested mode packet. Active `/deep:*` commands and native agent types remain as complementary surfaces over the same packets.\n87:Its legacy public surfaces, the `/deep:ai-council` command and the `ai-council` agent, intentionally keep the shorter `ai-council` key.\n105:- **ALWAYS** keep advisor projection maps hardcoded and drift-guarded against the registry; command mode routing is still hardcoded in the command files and does not resolve through `mode-registry.json`.\n107:- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity, whether a mode is reached via `Skill(system-deep-loop)`, a `/deep:*` command, or an agent.\n109:- **ALWAYS** keep `Skill(system-deep-loop)` hub routing, the `/deep:*` commands, and the agent types as complementary surfaces over the same packets — never let one surface fork per-mode logic out of its packet.\n126:- Commands: the active `/deep:*` commands under `.skilled/commands/deep/` (complementary surface).\n136:- `Skill(system-deep-loop[, hint])` reaches a mode, and the `/deep:*` commands and agent types still reach the same packets.\n149:- `Skill(system-deep-loop)` is the invokable hub; active `/deep:*` commands and the agent types (`deep-research`, `deep-review`, `ai-council`) are complementary surfaces over the same packets.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -rn \"command\" .skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md | head -8; echo \"---DEEP-HUB---\"; grep -n \"command\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/SKILL.md | head -8","time":{"start":1794476967423,"end":1794476967442}},"id":"prt_1cf29849e001OFyJZ5idu5i97a","sessionID":"ses_e30e8da22ffebDKB2cyvqFU701","messageID":"msg_1cf295e23001HdT4mgFPSOC7BA"}}
